      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Yes, fat bloom is specifically caused by unstable cocoa butter crystals (beta polymorphs) melting and recrystallizing into softer Form V or reverting to unstable forms, creating that opaque surface layer. When tempering goes wrong, the cocoa butter fails to stabilize into rigid Form V crystals, leading to a disordered molecular structure that softens and migrates to the surface. At a molecular level, this migration disrupts the glossy surface reflection, turning the smooth chocolate white and dull.",
